1
00:00:01,043 --> 00:00:02,748
Forty trillion dollars.

2
00:00:04,661 --> 00:00:07,966
That's how much the United States
government owes right now.

3
00:00:08,227 --> 00:00:11,236
More than $40 trillion,
and still climbing.

4
00:00:11,932 --> 00:00:18,048
In August 2026, America's debt crossed
$40 trillion for the first time in history.

5
00:00:18,309 --> 00:00:21,925
Just five months earlier,
it was $39 trillion.

6
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:25,175
And here's the part most
people don't realise.

7
00:00:25,436 --> 00:00:32,139
In the fiscal year that just ended, America
spent $1.14 trillion on interest alone.

8
00:00:32,574 --> 00:00:34,978
That's more than its entire military.

9
00:00:35,239 --> 00:00:36,573
More than Medicare.

10
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:39,015
Only Social Security costs more.

11
00:00:39,711 --> 00:00:41,266
Every single second,

12
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:46,785
around $36,000 of American tax money
disappears into interest payments.

13
00:00:47,046 --> 00:00:48,300
Not into roads.

14
00:00:48,561 --> 00:00:49,826
Not into schools.

15
00:00:50,218 --> 00:00:51,504
Just interest.

16
00:00:53,547 --> 00:00:54,972
This has happened before.

17
00:00:55,232 --> 00:01:00,008
Nearly 1,800 years ago,
the Roman Empire had its own money problem.

18
00:01:00,703 --> 00:01:05,409
To pay its soldiers and fund its wars,
Rome quietly watered down its money.

19
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:07,941
Over about two and a half centuries,

20
00:01:07,941 --> 00:01:12,672
its silver coins went from almost pure
silver to only a few percent silver.

21
00:01:13,368 --> 00:01:16,068
Prices soared, and trust
in the money collapsed.

22
00:01:16,329 --> 00:01:21,286
In the year 301, Emperor Diocletian
tried to cap prices by decree.

23
00:01:21,547 --> 00:01:22,637
It didn't work.

24
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:29,522
The Western Empire held on for almost
two more centuries, until the year 476.

25
00:01:29,783 --> 00:01:32,787
It didn't collapse in a day.
It weakened slowly,

26
00:01:32,787 --> 00:01:37,417
as its costs outran its income and its
money lost the trust of its people.

27
00:01:38,112 --> 00:01:39,643
America is not Rome.

28
00:01:39,904 --> 00:01:42,529
It borrows instead of debasing its coins,

29
00:01:42,529 --> 00:01:45,347
and its economy is
the largest in the world.

30
00:01:45,608 --> 00:01:50,090
Different tools, same question: how long
will the world keep trusting the money?

31
00:01:50,786 --> 00:01:52,631
So is this the beginning of the end?

32
00:01:52,892 --> 00:01:55,800
Or is it one of the most
overhyped fears in finance?

33
00:01:56,339 --> 00:01:59,874
Stay with me, because the answer
is more surprising than you think.

34
00:02:08,743 --> 00:02:09,797
Let's start simple.

35
00:02:10,336 --> 00:02:15,462
Every year, the US government spends money
on Social Security, Medicare, defence,

36
00:02:15,462 --> 00:02:19,070
roads, schools, and interest
on the debt it already owes.

37
00:02:19,766 --> 00:02:28,024
In fiscal year 2026, it spent $7.4 trillion.
But it collected only $5.4 trillion.

38
00:02:28,719 --> 00:02:29,671
Think about that.

39
00:02:29,932 --> 00:02:35,793
For every $100 the government spent,
it brought in only about $73.

40
00:02:36,489 --> 00:02:37,790
So it borrows the rest.

41
00:02:38,051 --> 00:02:40,392
Nearly $2 trillion in a single year.

42
00:02:40,653 --> 00:02:44,311
That's about $5.5 billion,
every single day.

43
00:02:45,007 --> 00:02:48,888
It borrows by selling Treasury
bonds to investors around the world,

44
00:02:48,888 --> 00:02:51,378
and those investors
lend America the money.

45
00:02:51,917 --> 00:02:54,791
Sounds fine, right?
Countries borrow all the time.

46
00:02:55,661 --> 00:02:57,676
But here's where it turns dangerous.

47
00:02:58,215 --> 00:02:59,984
That new borrowing adds to the debt.

48
00:03:00,244 --> 00:03:01,830
More debt means more interest.

49
00:03:02,091 --> 00:03:07,331
In just one year, America's interest
bill jumped by $115 billion.

50
00:03:07,592 --> 00:03:10,365
More interest means even
more borrowing next year.

51
00:03:10,625 --> 00:03:13,851
And when old bonds come due,
America doesn't pay them off.

52
00:03:14,112 --> 00:03:15,822
It borrows again to repay them.

53
00:03:16,518 --> 00:03:16,865
Today,

54
00:03:16,865 --> 00:03:21,491
more than one out of every five dollars
the government collects goes straight to

55
00:03:21,491 --> 00:03:22,011
interest.

56
00:03:22,707 --> 00:03:28,186
Split evenly, the debt now works out
to more than $115,000 for every man,

57
00:03:28,186 --> 00:03:30,213
woman and child in America.

58
00:03:30,909 --> 00:03:32,658
It's a machine that feeds itself.

59
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:35,675
And the faster it spins,
the harder it is to stop.

60
00:03:39,762 --> 00:03:44,629
Now, you might think, "Come on. It's America.
Nobody seriously doubts America."

61
00:03:45,168 --> 00:03:46,918
Except... they already have.

62
00:03:47,614 --> 00:03:48,661
In August 2011,

63
00:03:48,661 --> 00:03:54,457
a political fight over the debt ceiling
pushed the US so close to the edge that S&P

64
00:03:54,457 --> 00:03:59,624
stripped America of its perfect AAA
credit rating for the first time ever.

65
00:04:00,163 --> 00:04:03,351
In August 2023, Fitch downgraded it too.

66
00:04:03,890 --> 00:04:05,598
And in May 2025, Moody's,

67
00:04:05,598 --> 00:04:09,903
which had given America its top
rating for more than a century,

68
00:04:09,903 --> 00:04:11,474
finally cut it as well.

69
00:04:13,387 --> 00:04:15,983
For the first time in modern history,

70
00:04:15,983 --> 00:04:21,807
not one of the three major rating agencies
gives the United States a perfect score.

71
00:04:22,503 --> 00:04:24,044
And the bond market noticed.

72
00:04:24,583 --> 00:04:32,049
In August 2026, the interest rate
on America's 30-year bond climbed to 5.31%.

73
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:37,709
That's the highest since 2007,
on the eve of the global financial crisis.

74
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:43,994
Just days earlier, the Treasury had
to offer 5.22% to sell new 30-year bonds,

75
00:04:43,994 --> 00:04:47,750
the highest at one of those
auctions since 2001.

76
00:04:48,446 --> 00:04:52,749
In other words, the world is still lending
to America. But it's charging more.

77
00:04:53,288 --> 00:04:55,905
That doesn't mean collapse
is coming tomorrow.

78
00:04:55,905 --> 00:05:00,173
But it does mean the people whose job
is to judge risk are getting nervous.

79
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:06,550
So how would a real crisis actually start?

80
00:05:06,811 --> 00:05:08,052
There are three doors.

81
00:05:08,313 --> 00:05:09,732
And none of them are pleasant.

82
00:05:10,645 --> 00:05:13,221
Door number one: the investors walk away.

83
00:05:14,438 --> 00:05:19,074
Imagine one morning, global investors
simply say: "We'll still lend you money...

84
00:05:19,074 --> 00:05:21,392
but only at much higher interest rates."

85
00:05:22,087 --> 00:05:23,370
Bond prices crash.

86
00:05:23,631 --> 00:05:24,597
Yields shoot up.

87
00:05:24,858 --> 00:05:28,020
Suddenly every dollar
America borrows costs more,

88
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:31,440
every dollar it refinances
costs more, and mortgages,

89
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:35,311
car loans and business loans
all get more expensive with it.

90
00:05:36,007 --> 00:05:37,897
For families, this isn't abstract.

91
00:05:38,158 --> 00:05:40,363
Mortgage rates move with Treasury yields.

92
00:05:40,624 --> 00:05:43,396
On a 30-year mortgage of $400,000,

93
00:05:43,396 --> 00:05:49,102
every extra percentage point adds
roughly $260 to the monthly payment.

94
00:05:49,797 --> 00:05:53,365
And remember, interest is already
America's second-biggest bill.

95
00:05:53,626 --> 00:05:59,796
The Congressional Budget Office projects
it will reach $2.1 trillion a year by 2036,

96
00:05:59,796 --> 00:06:01,339
even without a panic.

97
00:06:02,035 --> 00:06:07,218
Think a sudden panic is far-fetched?
In 2022, it happened to the United Kingdom.

98
00:06:07,914 --> 00:06:09,630
On 23 September 2022,

99
00:06:09,630 --> 00:06:15,023
the British government announced
£45 billion of unfunded tax cuts.

100
00:06:15,284 --> 00:06:16,354
Investors panicked.

101
00:06:16,615 --> 00:06:19,778
The pound crashed to its lowest
level against the dollar in history.

102
00:06:20,039 --> 00:06:21,982
British bond markets went into freefall.

103
00:06:22,243 --> 00:06:25,128
Pension funds were pushed
to the edge of collapse,

104
00:06:25,128 --> 00:06:29,687
and the Bank of England had to pledge
up to £65 billion in an emergency rescue.

105
00:06:30,356 --> 00:06:33,334
Less than a month later,
the Prime Minister resigned.

106
00:06:35,247 --> 00:06:36,230
That was Britain.

107
00:06:36,491 --> 00:06:41,535
Now imagine it happening to the country
whose bonds the entire world depends on.

108
00:06:42,448 --> 00:06:44,992
Door number two: the dollar slowly melts.

109
00:06:46,209 --> 00:06:49,984
If the government leans on printing
money to handle its debt,

110
00:06:49,984 --> 00:06:51,903
the dollar quietly loses value.

111
00:06:52,164 --> 00:06:53,309
Prices rise.

112
00:06:53,570 --> 00:06:54,654
Savings shrink.

113
00:06:54,915 --> 00:06:58,484
And foreign investors start
asking a dangerous question:

114
00:06:58,484 --> 00:07:00,842
"Why are we holding so many dollars?"

115
00:07:01,537 --> 00:07:04,568
A weaker dollar also makes
imports more expensive.

116
00:07:04,829 --> 00:07:09,112
Foreign sellers want more dollars
for the same goods, so phones, cars,

117
00:07:09,112 --> 00:07:11,376
clothes and coffee can all cost more.

118
00:07:11,637 --> 00:07:15,407
Your paycheck buys less, even if
the number on it stays the same.

119
00:07:16,103 --> 00:07:21,287
This one doesn't arrive with a crash.
It creeps in. And by the time people notice,

120
00:07:21,287 --> 00:07:22,425
it's already deep.

121
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,050
Is it already happening? Slowly.

122
00:07:25,311 --> 00:07:26,622
Around the year 2000,

123
00:07:26,622 --> 00:07:31,365
more than 70% of the world's foreign-exchange
reserves were held in dollars.

124
00:07:31,626 --> 00:07:36,208
By mid-2026, that share
had slipped to 56.7%.

125
00:07:37,121 --> 00:07:40,179
Door number three: America
defaults... on purpose.

126
00:07:41,396 --> 00:07:42,998
This is the strangest one.

127
00:07:43,259 --> 00:07:45,917
America could have the money
and still fail to pay.

128
00:07:46,456 --> 00:07:46,997
How?

129
00:07:47,258 --> 00:07:48,236
Politics.

130
00:07:48,932 --> 00:07:51,859
By law, America can only
borrow up to a set limit.

131
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:55,743
Right now, that limit is $41.1 trillion.

132
00:07:56,004 --> 00:07:59,397
And America is already within
about a trillion dollars of it.

133
00:07:59,658 --> 00:08:04,632
Independent estimates suggest the ceiling
could be hit sometime in 2027.

134
00:08:05,328 --> 00:08:08,793
If Congress and the White House
can't agree to raise it in time,

135
00:08:08,793 --> 00:08:10,200
payments could be delayed.

136
00:08:10,461 --> 00:08:15,364
That could include Social Security checks,
military pay and veterans' benefits.

137
00:08:15,625 --> 00:08:19,116
And the moment the world's
"safest" asset misses a payment,

138
00:08:19,116 --> 00:08:20,714
global markets could panic.

139
00:08:21,253 --> 00:08:23,166
Not because America is broke.

140
00:08:23,427 --> 00:08:25,684
But because America chose to stumble.

141
00:08:27,597 --> 00:08:29,217
Now, here's something important.

142
00:08:29,478 --> 00:08:31,675
A crisis is not the same as collapse.

143
00:08:31,936 --> 00:08:35,774
A country can go through a brutal
financial shock and still survive.

144
00:08:36,035 --> 00:08:40,854
But the Congressional Budget Office itself
has warned that rising debt raises the

145
00:08:40,854 --> 00:08:42,996
risk of exactly this kind of crisis,

146
00:08:42,996 --> 00:08:45,911
and leaves less room
to fight the next emergency.

147
00:08:49,998 --> 00:08:53,430
Okay. If all this is true...
why is America still standing?

148
00:08:53,969 --> 00:08:56,605
Because it holds some of the
strongest cards in the world.

149
00:08:57,301 --> 00:08:58,881
First, the dollar is still king.

150
00:08:59,142 --> 00:09:01,363
Even after that slow decline,

151
00:09:01,363 --> 00:09:07,798
the dollar makes up 56.7% of global
foreign-exchange reserves, according to the IMF.

152
00:09:08,059 --> 00:09:12,148
The euro, its closest rival,
sits at about 20.6%.

153
00:09:12,409 --> 00:09:13,774
Nothing else comes close.

154
00:09:14,470 --> 00:09:17,725
Second, America has a giant,
productive economy,

155
00:09:17,725 --> 00:09:22,743
worth more than $30 trillion a year,
with huge companies, huge innovation,

156
00:09:22,743 --> 00:09:24,303
and a massive tax base.

157
00:09:24,564 --> 00:09:29,216
Even last year, tax revenue
rose by $169 billion.

158
00:09:29,912 --> 00:09:32,524
Third, America borrows
in its own currency.

159
00:09:32,784 --> 00:09:36,320
It never has to hunt for some
foreign currency to pay its bills.

160
00:09:36,581 --> 00:09:40,400
That alone protects it from the trap
that has broken many other countries.

161
00:09:41,095 --> 00:09:44,704
And fourth, US Treasuries are
the backbone of global finance.

162
00:09:44,965 --> 00:09:48,685
Banks, governments and funds all
use them as their safest asset.

163
00:09:48,946 --> 00:09:52,250
So far, every time America
has put its bonds up for sale,

164
00:09:52,250 --> 00:09:54,278
the world has shown up to buy them.

165
00:09:54,973 --> 00:09:56,418
So America is safe, right?

166
00:09:58,175 --> 00:09:59,139
Not quite.

167
00:09:59,834 --> 00:10:03,919
Because every one of those advantages
depends on one thing: trust.

168
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:06,207
And trust doesn't disappear slowly.

169
00:10:06,468 --> 00:10:08,345
It disappears all at once.

170
00:10:12,432 --> 00:10:17,384
People love comparing America to the
Roman Empire. And it's easy to see why.

171
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:21,955
But a modern collapse wouldn't
look like barbarians at the gates.

172
00:10:21,955 --> 00:10:24,400
It would look like a slow chain reaction.

173
00:10:25,096 --> 00:10:26,795
Deficits that never stop.

174
00:10:27,056 --> 00:10:34,715
The CBO projects the yearly deficit growing
from about $2 trillion today to $3.1

175
00:10:34,715 --> 00:10:36,342
trillion by 2036.

176
00:10:36,603 --> 00:10:38,408
Interest rates that keep climbing.

177
00:10:38,668 --> 00:10:43,933
Politicians forced into impossible
choices: raise taxes, cut benefits,

178
00:10:43,933 --> 00:10:45,437
or borrow even more.

179
00:10:45,698 --> 00:10:46,992
Then slower growth.

180
00:10:47,253 --> 00:10:48,482
Stubborn inflation.

181
00:10:48,742 --> 00:10:50,982
Investors losing faith in Treasuries.

182
00:10:51,808 --> 00:10:54,506
And here's a date to remember: 2030.

183
00:10:54,767 --> 00:10:57,256
That's when, in the CBO's projections,

184
00:10:57,256 --> 00:11:01,318
America's debt breaks the record
set right after World War II.

185
00:11:02,014 --> 00:11:03,793
And finally, the quiet part.

186
00:11:04,054 --> 00:11:07,623
America's financial superpower
status starts slipping away.

187
00:11:07,884 --> 00:11:08,863
Not in a day.

188
00:11:09,124 --> 00:11:14,034
But year by year, until one day
people realise the world has moved on.

189
00:11:15,947 --> 00:11:17,689
Is that guaranteed? No.

190
00:11:17,950 --> 00:11:22,909
Smart reforms, stronger growth, or tough
decisions could still turn this around.

191
00:11:23,170 --> 00:11:27,271
But every year that nothing changes,
the window gets a little smaller.

192
00:11:31,358 --> 00:11:33,740
So, will America collapse
because of its debt?

193
00:11:34,436 --> 00:11:35,827
Here's the honest answer.

194
00:11:36,088 --> 00:11:40,335
There are three very different
futures: America going bankrupt,

195
00:11:40,335 --> 00:11:43,638
America going through
a painful financial crisis,

196
00:11:43,638 --> 00:11:48,424
or America slowly losing its crown
as the world's financial superpower.

197
00:11:48,964 --> 00:11:50,596
The first is unlikely.

198
00:11:50,857 --> 00:11:52,371
The second is possible.

199
00:11:52,632 --> 00:11:53,587
And the third?

200
00:11:53,978 --> 00:11:55,681
That's the one worth watching.

201
00:11:56,376 --> 00:12:03,291
The CBO projects the debt held by the public
rising from about 101% of the economy

202
00:12:03,291 --> 00:12:09,363
this year to 120% by 2036, higher
than at any point in American history.

203
00:12:09,624 --> 00:12:14,761
Interest costs are on track to nearly
double, to $2.1 trillion a year.

204
00:12:15,021 --> 00:12:17,960
Nobody can tell you the exact
date when things break.

205
00:12:18,221 --> 00:12:19,645
But the direction is clear.

206
00:12:20,340 --> 00:12:24,763
For most Americans, the danger won't
arrive as a headline about collapse.

207
00:12:25,024 --> 00:12:28,802
It would show up quietly:
in pricier loans, pricier imports,

208
00:12:28,802 --> 00:12:31,825
and less room in the budget
for everything else.

209
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:35,791
The real question isn't
whether America can borrow.

210
00:12:36,138 --> 00:12:39,188
It's how long the world will
keep believing it should.

211
00:12:40,144 --> 00:12:41,269
So what do you think?

212
00:12:41,530 --> 00:12:46,678
Is $40 trillion a ticking time bomb,
or a problem America will fix in time?

213
00:12:46,939 --> 00:12:48,569
Drop your answer in the comments.

214
00:12:49,108 --> 00:12:52,197
And if you want to know who
actually owns all this debt,

215
00:12:52,197 --> 00:12:56,445
or whether the dollar could lose its
throne, tell me which one you want next.

216
00:12:56,706 --> 00:12:58,607
Because that story is even scarier.
